#+title: relationship-annotator and discourse-graphs: what each does

=relationship-annotator= (formerly =vulpea-hierarchy=, in =libraries/modules/custom/sp-relationship-annotator.el=) and the =discourse-graphs= package (=dg-=, [[https://github.com/LuciusChen/discourse-graphs][LuciusChen/discourse-graphs]]) both read typed relations between notes. They started from different needs. =discourse-graphs= is a tool for writing an argument: you create typed nodes, link them, and ask what is missing. =vulpea-hierarchy= was a viewer for the parent/child tree of a Zettelkasten. =relationship-annotator= keeps the viewer and has taken over the reading, checking and counting of =discourse-graphs=, so the package is no longer needed for those. This page says what is the same, what differs, and what was left out on purpose.

Source of the package side: =discourse-graphs.el= (3299 lines) and =discourse-graphs-ui.el= (450 lines), read as of the version cloned for this port. Line numbers below refer to that file. The literate source of this module is in [[file:tangles/custom/sp-relationship-annotator.org][sp-relationship-annotator.org]].

* In short

| Question                      | =discourse-graphs=                                          | =relationship-annotator=                                            |
|-------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Where are nodes?              | Org headings with =:ID:= and =:DG_TYPE:=, or files with =#+dg_type:= | Any vulpea note (one note, one file)                                |
| Where is the data kept?       | Its own SQLite cache, filled by scanning =dg-directories=   | vulpea's database; nothing is scanned or stored by the module       |
| How is a node typed?          | =DG_TYPE= property or =#+dg_type:= keyword                  | =DG_TYPE= property, a =type= metadata field, or a file tag (configurable) |
| How is a relation stored?     | One property per type on the source: =:DG_SUPPORTS: id id=  | Any number of /sources/ per relation: metadata, inverse metadata, property, inverse property |
| Which relations exist?        | =supports=, =opposes=, =informs=, =answers=                 | The same four, plus =parent=, plus any you declare                  |
| Is the tree special?          | No, all relations are alike                                 | Yes: =parent= builds the hierarchy that every view is cut from      |
| Pattern check                 | Prompts when you create a link that doesn't fit             | A report and a warning sign in the graph; nothing is refused        |
| Attributes                    | Formulas per type, shown as an overlay after the heading    | Formulas per type, shown as a badge after the title in the graph    |
| Argument gaps                 | Dynamic blocks in an Org file                               | One review buffer                                                   |
| Creating nodes and links      | Commands, a transient menu, extraction of regions           | Not ported (see below)                                              |
| Views                         | Context buffer beside the note, and a web UI over websockets | Interactive browser graph, two outlines, a Graphviz SVG             |
| Dependencies                  | SQLite, =websocket= (for the UI), =transient=, a Next.js export    | =vulpea=, =vui= (trial outline only), Graphviz (SVG only)            |

* What they share

** The relation model

An edge reads "subject relation object": =evidence supports claim=. The subject holds the information (in =discourse-graphs=, the property is on the subject), the inverse is found by looking backwards. Both name the relation from the object's side too (="Supported By"=), and both use that name in formulas. The default relations, their colours, their inverse labels and the types they connect are the same:

| Relation   | Inverse label  | Subject  | Object   |
|------------+----------------+----------+----------|
| =supports= | Supported By   | evidence | claim    |
| =opposes=  | Opposed By     | evidence | claim    |
| =informs=  | Informed By    | source   | evidence |
| =answers=  | Answered By    | claim    | question |

In the package these are =dg-relation-types= and =dg-relation-patterns=. Here both live in one place, =relationship-annotator-relations=, and a relation also carries its direction on the page, its colour, and its sources. Notes written for the package keep working: the property names (=DG_SUPPORTS=, =DG_TYPE=) are the same.

** The node types

=question=, =claim=, =evidence= and =source=, with a short code, a colour and a description. In the package the type is a string stored in the cache. Here =relationship-annotator-node-types= keeps them and a note's type is read when needed.

** Pattern violations are allowed

A relation with no listed types accepts anything. A pattern that doesn't fit is not an error in either tool. The package asks "Create anyway?" when you make such a link. This module reports it.

** The formula language

Both parse =+ - * /=, parentheses, numbers, and terms in braces: ={count:Supported By:evidence}=, ={sum:Supported By:claim:robustness}=, ={avg:...}=. Both let a formula use another attribute of the related notes, and both return 0 for a division by zero. The default attributes (=evidence-score=, =robustness=, =total-support=, =answer-count= and so on) are the package's.

** The argument checks

=no-support=, =no-source= and =unanswered-opposition= for a claim, and the strengths of an answer (=unsupported=, =challenged=, =contested=, =supported=, =unknown=), in the same order of testing.

* What differs

** Storage and scanning

The package scans every Org file under =dg-directories=, opens each with =find-file-noselect=, and writes what it finds to SQLite (tables =nodes=, =relations=, =node_files=). It rebuilds incrementally by file modification time. That is about 700 of its 3300 lines. vulpea already indexes the notes and their metadata and properties, so this module doesn't scan. The cost is that it depends on what vulpea indexes: metadata is indexed by key, but properties are not, so a property source reads every note (=vulpea-db-query= with a predicate). A vault with many notes and a property source pays for that on each run.

** Heading nodes and file nodes

The package has two kinds of node: an Org heading with =:ID:= and =:DG_TYPE:= properties, and a file with a header. =relationship-annotator= has one kind, the vulpea note, which is a file or a heading with an ID.

Two details of the package were not carried over because they look like gaps in it. File nodes can't have a relation note (=:DG_SUPPORTS_NOTE:=), and the package ignores Denote =#+filetags= when typing a node, although it writes the Denote keyword when it creates one. Here a file tag can be the type.

** The tree

The package has no tree: a graph in its web UI is a set of nodes and links. =vulpea-hierarchy= was built around the =parents= metadata, and =parent= is still the one relation that is special here. It gives every view its starting point: you choose a note and a depth, and the page shows the tree below it, with the other relations as links between the notes in view. The package shows everything it knows.

** Layout and the graph

The package's web UI is a Next.js export served from Emacs on port 8080, with a websocket on port 35904 that sends the nodes and links and receives "open this node". The page here is one self-contained HTML file with the layout library inside it: it needs no server, no =websocket= package, and no network. In exchange, it is a snapshot: to see a change, run the command again.

The layout also differs in what you can say about it. Each relation has a =:direction= (up, down, left, right or an angle) that says where its object goes, and parents always go above their children. Boxes take the colour of their file tags, or of their type. A click recentres the graph. The package's UI gets each node's type and each link's style (solid or dashed) in its data; it does not take layout rules.

** Anomaly check

The logic is the same function. The package runs it when you create a link, and over the whole cache for its anomaly dynamic block. Here it runs over every edge when the page is built or the review buffer is opened. One difference: a note with no type is never checked here, because a vulpea vault has many notes that were never classified. The package only knows typed notes, so the case doesn't come up there.

** Formulas

Three fixes were made while porting:

- The package parses with a global list of tokens, so a formula can't be parsed while another is. The parser here keeps its tokens in a closure.
- The package keeps a table of the four default relations for formulas. Here the names come from =relationship-annotator-relations=, so a relation you declare can be used in a formula.
- The package has no guard against a formula that uses its own attribute and recurses forever. Here it stops with a message. The package's cache also treats a cached 0 as a miss, and this one doesn't.

The overlay differs too: the package picks a format per attribute name (such as =[+3/-1]=). The badge here is the plain number of the attribute named by =:overlay=.

** Reports

The package writes reports as Org dynamic blocks (=dg-overview=, =dg-argument-gaps=, =dg-unanswered-opposition=, =dg-synthesis=, =dg-relation-anomalies=, =dg-query=, =dg-node-index=) and has =dg-synthesis-open= and =dg-query-builder= to make them. This module has one buffer, =relationship-annotator-review=, with the anomalies, the claims with gaps, and the questions with the strength of each answer.

* What was not ported

| =discourse-graphs=                                              | Why it was left out                                                                |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| SQLite cache, scanning, incremental rebuild (L252-611, 1791-1985) | vulpea is the database                                                             |
| Web UI, websocket and HTTP servers (=discourse-graphs-ui.el=)   | The browser page is self-contained and static                                      |
| Creating nodes, converting headings, =dg-link=, =dg-remove-relation=, extracting regions | This module reads. Writing metadata should go through vulpea; see the design considerations |
| Context buffer that follows the cursor, overlays in the buffer   | The package's overlays work on headings in the current buffer. Not started          |
| =dg:= link type, =dg-goto-node=                                  | =id:= links and vulpea's own selection do this                                     |
| Dynamic blocks and the transient menu                            | Replaced by the review buffer                                                      |
| =dg-validate= (dangling ids, orphans, missing files)             | Not started. A dangling id already shows as =<missing ID>= in the views            |

"Not started" means not decided against. They are the obvious next steps if the module should also be where you write relations. That question belongs to the design considerations section that follows in the next change.

* What =vulpea-hierarchy= had that the package does not

- The scoped views: every command asks for a starting note and a depth, and nothing draws the whole vault.
- Several sources for one relation, so notes written as =parents ::= on a child and notes written as =children ::= on a parent give the same edge once.
- The native Emacs outline (and its =vui= trial), and the Graphviz SVG export with its file naming.
- Tag colours, wrapped labels in the page and in the SVG, and recentring by click.

